The time it takes for a photon to travel from the Sun to Earth can be calculated using the formula:
$t = \frac{d}{c}$
where $t$ is the time, $d$ is the distance between the Sun and Earth, and $c$ is the speed of light.
The average distance between the Sun and Earth is approximately 93 million miles, or 149.6 million kilometers. The speed of light is approximately 299,792,458 meters per second.
Converting the distance to meters, we get:
$d = 149.6 \times 10^6 \text{ km} \times 10^3 \text{ m/km} = 1.496 \times 10^{11} \text{ m}$
Plugging in the values, we get:
$t = \frac{1.496 \times 10^{11} \text{ m}}{299,792,458 \text{ m/s}} \approx 499.005 \text{ seconds}$
Therefore, it takes approximately 499 seconds, or 8 minutes and 19 seconds, for a photon of light to travel from the Sun to Earth.

