2. Create a new PDO object to connect to the database using the appropriate credentials.
3. Prepare a SQL query to extract the relevant data from the NHANES database. This query will likely involve joining multiple tables and filtering by specific criteria (e.g. respiratory conditions and air pollution exposure).
4. Execute the query using the PDO object and retrieve the results as an array of associative arrays.
Here is an example code snippet that demonstrates how to connect to the NHANES database and extract data related to respiratory conditions and air pollution exposure using PHP:
```
<?php
// Set database credentials
$host = "localhost";
$dbname = "nhanes";
$username = "root";
$password = "password";
// Connect to the database using PDO
try {
    $pdo = new PDO("mysql:host=$host;dbname=$dbname", $username, $password);
    $pdo->setAttribute(PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE, PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION);
} catch (PDOException $e) {
    die("Failed to connect to database: " . $e->getMessage());
}
// Prepare SQL query to extract data
$query = "SELECT * FROM respiratory_conditions
          JOIN air_pollution_exposure ON respiratory_conditions.id = air_pollution_exposure.condition_id
          WHERE air_pollution_exposure.exposure_level > 10";
// Execute query and retrieve results
try {
    $stmt = $pdo->prepare($query);
    $stmt->execute();
    $results = $stmt->fetchAll(PDO::FETCH_ASSOC);
} catch (PDOException $e) {
    die("Failed to execute query: " . $e->getMessage());
}
// Print results
print_r($results);
?>
```
This code assumes that you have a database named "nhanes" with tables named "respiratory_conditions" and "air_pollution_exposure" that contain the relevant data. You will need to modify the SQL query to match the specific table and column names in your database. Additionally, you may need to adjust the criteria for filtering the data based on your research needs.

