	<h2>Historical Background of the Schlieffen Plan</h2>
	<p>The Schlieffen Plan was a military strategy developed by German General Alfred von Schlieffen in 1905. The plan was designed to deal with the perceived threat of a two-front war against France and Russia. The plan called for a rapid and decisive invasion of France through Belgium, followed by a swift turn to the east to defeat the Russian army. The Schlieffen Plan was based on the assumption that Germany could not fight a two-front war and that France would be defeated quickly, allowing Germany to focus its efforts on the eastern front.</p>

	<h2>Strategic Objectives of the Schlieffen Plan</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>The first objective was to defeat France quickly and decisively.</li>
		<li>The second objective was to avoid a two-front war.</li>
		<li>The third objective was to avoid a long and costly war.</li>
