Different speaking styles in public speaking require different skills and techniques. Here's a breakdown of impromptu, extemporaneous, and scripted speaking styles:
1. Impromptu speaking: This is when a speaker is given a topic and has to speak on it without any preparation. The key skills required for impromptu speaking are the ability to think on your feet, organize your thoughts quickly, and communicate clearly.
2. Extemporaneous speaking: This is when a speaker has some time to prepare, but not enough to memorize a speech word-for-word. The key skills required for extemporaneous speaking are the ability to organize your thoughts, speak naturally and conversationally, and maintain eye contact with your audience.
3. Scripted speaking: This is when a speaker has written out their speech word-for-word and memorized it. The key skills required for scripted speaking are the ability to deliver the speech with conviction and enthusiasm, maintain eye contact with the audience, and use body language to emphasize key points.
Psychological factors such as fear and confidence can greatly influence public speaking. Fear of public speaking, also known as glossophobia, is one of the most common fears. Techniques to overcome fear and boost confidence include practicing your speech, visualizing a successful outcome, and focusing on your breathing.
Virtual communication tools like video conferencing and social media have become increasingly important in public speaking. Strategies to adapt to these technological advancements include familiarizing yourself with the platform you'll be using, testing your equipment beforehand, and using engaging visuals and multimedia to keep your audience's attention.
